{
  "content": "**Sequencing / spec construction**\n- Build the task spec incrementally from user messages only, in order. Treat each new user turn as a constraint update, not a fresh standalone task, and merge it with the prior valid constraints before judging the assistant.\n- Use the latest user message as authoritative for scope changes, but preserve earlier user constraints that remain active. If the user narrows, adds, or restates requirements, intersect the old spec with the new one rather than replacing it wholesale.\n- Separate a true task-shape change from a simple refinement. If the user changes the deliverable type, granularity, aggregation level, or ordering, re-derive the spec from that point forward; if they only add a constraint, update the existing spec cumulatively.\n- Compact away assistant-added speculation, detours, and unsupported assumptions when building the merged spec. Only carry forward constraints grounded in user messages and validated by the conversation context.\n- Prefer the smallest faithful spec supported by the latest cumulative user intent. Optimize the analysis for the final request shape, not the broad theme or earlier exploratory branches.\n\n**Constraint decomposition**\n- Track content requirements and output shape separately: target entities, required fields, filters, grouping/aggregation, ordering, and formatting are distinct dimensions. This prevents missing cases where the right substance is returned in the wrong form.\n- Distinguish row/entity selection from projection. A response can satisfy the right filter or relationship logic and still fail if it returns the wrong columns, granularity, or answer type.\n- Treat exact scope as authoritative. If the user asks for a specific subset, representation, or property of returned items, do not weaken it into a broader semantically related answer.\n- When the user asks whether something exists, is present, or is the minimum/maximum, do not silently reinterpret the task as counting, summarizing, enumerating, or selecting a different level of aggregation unless the user changes the deliverable type.\n- Preserve the full accumulated set of requested fields or attributes when the user adds them incrementally. Later additions expand the required output; they do not replace earlier field requirements unless the user clearly resets them.\n\n**Diagnostic patterns**\n- Check both missing and surplus elements against the merged spec. Extra fields, extra alternatives, extra summaries, stale sorting, and unnecessary verbosity can all be divergence, not just omissions.\n- Treat ordering requirements as first-class constraints. A correct content set in the wrong order is still a mismatch.\n- Watch for aggregation-level drift: list vs count, detailed rows vs summary, per-item vs overall aggregate, or \u201cshow\u201d vs \u201chow many.\u201d A logically related response can still be the wrong deliverable type.\n- Flag over-specification when the assistant adds context, variants, counts, placeholders, or broader structure that the user did not request. Extra detail is not harmless if it changes the answer shape or obscures the requested one.\n- Recognize neighboring-problem answers. A response may be internally correct and still fail if it answers a broader, narrower, or adjacent question rather than the user\u2019s actual one.\n- When the user progressively reveals more specific constraints, re-evaluate earlier \u201cmostly right\u201d reasoning against the full updated spec instead of inheriting it automatically.\n- If the user\u2019s wording changes from exploratory to concrete, compare the assistant\u2019s output shape explicitly against the requested one: exact fields, exact entity scope, exact aggregation, and exact ordering.\n- If a later user constraint is omitted, treat that as a direct contradiction of the updated spec even when earlier parts of the response were sensible.\n- If the user asks for a property of the returned entities, verify that the assistant returns that property explicitly, not just a proxy like identifiers or names when the requested attribute is different.\n\n**Common pitfalls to avoid**\n- Do not let earlier partially correct reasoning mask a later mismatch. A response can satisfy an intermediate step while still failing the final merged user objective.\n- Do not preserve alternative interpretations after the user has clarified. Once ambiguity is resolved, continue with one merged spec, not a menu of possibilities.\n- Do not reward \u201cdirectionally correct\u201d responses if they omit a required field or return the wrong output form. Partial correctness is not enough when the final deliverable is structurally wrong.\n- Do not overgeneralize from a broader acceptable condition when the user asked for an exact subset, exact enumeration, or exact ordering.\n- Do not anchor on the conversation theme. Compare the final answer to the latest cumulative user intent, not to an earlier framing or the general topic.\n- Do not confuse internally consistent reasoning with actual alignment. A response can be logically sound and still miss the user\u2019s final constraint set.\n- Do not treat tie-breaking or limiting as required unless the conversation actually establishes that exactly one row is needed. If the user has not asked for a single winner, multiple minimum/maximum matches may still satisfy the task.\n- Do not let earlier assistant assumptions become part of the compacted context. If they were not user-stated or schema-grounded, strip them out before evaluation.\n\n**Successful analysis strategies**\n- Use the latest user message to update the task, then re-check the assistant against the merged cumulative spec. This catches both newly introduced constraints and stale earlier assumptions.\n- Separate reusable correctness from final output shape. The assistant may use the right underlying logic but still need correction if the projection, granularity, or ordering no longer matches.\n- When constraints narrow over time, preserve the aligned core and trim away obsolete breadth. This keeps the analysis focused on what remains required, not on discarded branches.\n- In the final assessment, distinguish true contradictions from merely extraneous additions so the analyzer highlights actionable mismatches rather than harmless implementation choices.\n- Prefer diagnosing whether the assistant returned the right entity and attributes before judging stylistic details. Presentation issues matter only when they affect the requested answer shape.",
  "version": 4,
  "history": [
    "",
    "<updated_cheatsheet>\n**Sequencing / task-spec construction**\n- Start by building the task spec from the user\u2019s messages in order, but treat each later user turn as a constraint update that can narrow, replace, or supersede earlier interpretations. The final spec should reflect the latest accumulated user intent, not a stale broad reading.\n- Once the user has provided enough detail to fully specify the request, collapse exploratory context into the final actionable requirement set. Do not keep multiple alternative interpretations alive after the user has resolved the ambiguity.\n- When the user says \u201calso include X,\u201d \u201cinclude Y too,\u201d or gives an additional field/filter, update the spec cumulatively and verify the assistant against the full combined set. Earlier requirements remain active unless the user explicitly overwrites them.\n- If the user later narrows the objective, overwrite superseded intent rather than carrying forward broader earlier goals. The analyzer should optimize for the final request shape, not the whole dialogue history.\n\n**Constraint comparison strategy**\n- Compare the assistant\u2019s last response against the final user message with a constraint-by-constraint diff. Check both missing elements and surplus elements: wrong output fields, extra filters, unnecessary ordering, and added defensive logic can all be divergence.\n- Prefer exact constraint matching over semantic approximation. If the user gives an exact value set or exact inclusion/exclusion rule, do not weaken it into a broader range or \u201cclose enough\u201d category.\n- Classify mismatches precisely as superset, subset, sibling, or over-specified relative to the user\u2019s accumulated request. This helps distinguish true contradictions from merely unnecessary complexity.\n- When the user narrows from a broad condition to a precise subset, verify that the assistant matches the narrowed intent exactly rather than preserving the earlier generalization.\n\n**Diagnostic patterns**\n- Treat extra columns, extra sorting, summary fields, or schema-valid but unrequested details as potential overreach unless the user explicitly asked for them. Distinguish output requirements from implementation convenience.\n- Distinguish \u201cwhat the user wants to show/return\u201d from incidental filtering or robustness logic. Additional predicates or schema-handling steps may be acceptable internally, but they should not be counted as fulfilling the user\u2019s request if the returned shape is wrong.\n- When the task is about entities that \u201chave,\u201d \u201coperate,\u201d or \u201cactually satisfy\u201d some event/relationship, ensure the analysis checks for evidence in the relevant relationship data, not just static existence in a dimension-like source.\n- If the assistant\u2019s response is semantically correct but more detailed than requested, label it as unnecessary complexity or over-specification rather than a full contradiction. This keeps the analysis precise about what truly needs correction.\n- Distinguish \u201cneeds more filtering\u201d from \u201cneeds a different projection.\u201d Many failures are not about which rows qualify, but about returning the wrong granularity, columns, or answer type.\n\n**Common pitfalls to avoid**\n- Do not let earlier partially-correct reasoning mask a later mismatch. A response can satisfy an intermediate step while still failing the final user objective.\n- Do not preserve alternative interpretations after the user has clarified or narrowed the request. Once resolved, ambiguity should be treated as resolved.\n- Do not overgeneralize from a broader acceptable condition when the user has asked for an exact subset or exact enumeration.\n- Do not treat assistant questions or menu-like clarification attempts as progress when the user has already provided sufficient detail. Repeated branching in a fully specified exchange is itself a divergence signal.\n- Do not confuse acceptable schema-valid extra fields with required answer content. If the user only asks for a name/identifier list, returning additional attributes may be noise unless explicitly requested.\n- Do not judge only the immediately preceding turn; in incremental disclosure settings, the final user wording can retroactively change what \u201ccorrect\u201d means.\n\n**Successful analysis strategies**\n- Preserve only the assistant moves that remain valid under the final spec; discard earlier reasoning that no longer matches the narrowed request.\n- Use the final user message as the authoritative constraint update while still carrying forward any earlier user requirements that have not been superseded.\n- For shard-style or incremental disclosure conversations, compact the history into one updated task spec before evaluating the assistant\u2019s latest output. This prevents stale broad interpretations from biasing the diagnosis.\n- Check whether the conversation has reached a fully specified query. If so, expect direct completion rather than more menus, clarification, or exploratory branching.\n- When the user\u2019s wording shifts from broad exploration to a concrete target, explicitly note whether the assistant matched the new target shape: entity list vs summary, records vs counts, minimal output vs expanded report.\n- Favor the narrowest faithful task spec supported by the user\u2019s latest explicit request. This improves detection of both missing requirements and unnecessary additions.\n</updated_cheatsheet>",
    "**Sequencing / spec construction**\n- Build the task spec only from user messages, in order, and treat each later user turn as a constraint update. Later turns may narrow, replace, or supersede earlier interpretations, so the final spec should reflect the latest merged user intent rather than the earliest broad reading.\n- After each user refinement, ask whether it changes the **task shape** or only adds a constraint. If it changes the final deliverable type, projection, ordering, or granularity, re-derive the spec from that point forward instead of blending old and new shapes.\n- Once the user has resolved ambiguity, collapse exploratory context into one definitive requirement set. Do not keep multiple interpretations alive after the user has already clarified what they want.\n- If the user says \u201calso include X,\u201d \u201cinclude Y too,\u201d or adds another field/filter/condition, update the spec cumulatively and verify the assistant against the combined set. Earlier requirements remain active unless explicitly overwritten.\n- If the user later narrows the objective, overwrite superseded intent rather than carrying forward the broader earlier goal. The analyzer should optimize for the final request shape, not the full dialogue history.\n- In iterative or edit-style conversations, preserve only the stable user constraints needed to continue; discard assistant-added assumptions and stale broad interpretations.\n\n**Constraint comparison strategy**\n- Compare the assistant\u2019s last response against the final merged user spec with a constraint-by-constraint diff. Check both missing elements and surplus elements: wrong output fields, extra filtering, stale ordering, and unnecessary verbosity can all be divergence.\n- Distinguish **what must be shown/returned** from **what qualifies the result**. The output shape and the eligibility logic are separate; a response can get the right items but still fail by returning the wrong projection or level of detail.\n- Prefer exact constraint matching over semantic approximation. If the user specifies an exact subset, exact inclusion/exclusion rule, exact field, or exact order, do not weaken it into \u201cclose enough.\u201d\n- Treat the latest user message as a constraint update, not a new open-ended task by default. Reconcile it with prior user requests to determine whether it narrows, restates, or overrides earlier requirements.\n- Classify mismatches precisely: supersets, subsets, siblings, and over-specified outputs should be distinguished so the analyzer can separate true contradictions from merely unnecessary complexity.\n- When the task evolves across turns, validate the final response only against the latest merged spec. Earlier exploratory interpretations should not be carried forward if later user messages narrow the scope.\n\n**Diagnostic patterns**\n- Flag extra columns, extra summaries, extra sorting, or schema-valid but unrequested details as potential overreach unless the user explicitly asked for them. Output projection mismatches are often as important as filtering mismatches.\n- Treat sort-order requests as first-class requirements. A stale ordering criterion is a concrete mismatch even if the selected content is otherwise correct.\n- When the user asks for a specific column or entity, prefer that exact requested representation over a fancier substitute that changes the answer shape, even if it is semantically related.\n- Distinguish \u201cmembership in a relationship\u201d from \u201ccounting or summarizing relationships.\u201d If the user only asks whether something exists or belongs, count-based or report-style responses may be unnecessary and can obscure the intended task.\n- When the task concerns entities that \u201chave,\u201d \u201coperate,\u201d \u201chost,\u201d or otherwise actually satisfy a relation, ensure the analysis checks the relevant relationship data, not just static presence in a dimension-like source.\n- Separate row-selection logic from projection logic. A response may satisfy the right filter or join pattern but still fail because the returned granularity, columns, or answer type is wrong.\n- If the assistant\u2019s response is semantically correct but more detailed than requested, classify it as over-specification or unnecessary complexity rather than a full contradiction.\n- Recognize when a query is logically equivalent but still not faithful to the user\u2019s requested output shape or constraints; equivalence of intent does not eliminate mismatched deliverables.\n\n**Common pitfalls to avoid**\n- Do not let earlier partially correct reasoning mask a later mismatch. A response can satisfy an intermediate step while still failing the final user objective.\n- Do not preserve alternative interpretations after the user has clarified or narrowed the request. Once resolved, ambiguity should be treated as resolved.\n- Do not overgeneralize from a broader acceptable condition when the user has asked for a precise subset or exact enumeration.\n- Do not treat assistant questions, menu-like clarification attempts, or exploratory branching as progress when the user has already provided sufficient detail. That usually signals drift, not helpfulness.\n- Do not confuse acceptable extra internal logic with acceptable extra answer content. Robustness steps may be fine inside the reasoning, but they do not justify returning a broader or different result than requested.\n- Do not judge only the immediately preceding turn. In incremental disclosure settings, the final user wording can retroactively change what \u201ccorrect\u201d means.\n- Do not carry forward broad earlier framing when the user has reduced the request to a minimal target. Anchoring drift often appears as stale summary/detail that keeps reappearing after the user has simplified the ask.\n\n**Successful analysis strategies**\n- Preserve only the assistant moves that remain valid under the final spec; discard earlier reasoning that no longer matches the narrowed request.\n- Use the final user message as the authoritative update while still carrying forward any earlier user requirements that have not been superseded.\n- For shard-style, edit-style, or incremental disclosure conversations, compact the history into one updated task spec before evaluating the assistant\u2019s latest output. This prevents stale broad interpretations from biasing the diagnosis.\n- Check whether the conversation has reached a fully specified query. If so, expect direct completion rather than menus, clarification, or exploratory branching.\n- When the user\u2019s wording shifts from broad exploration to a concrete target, explicitly compare the assistant\u2019s output shape against the requested one: entity list vs summary, records vs counts, minimal output vs expanded report.\n- Favor the narrowest faithful task spec supported by the user\u2019s latest explicit request. This improves detection of both missing requirements and unnecessary additions.\n- In the final \u201cwhat needs to change\u201d assessment, separate true contradictions from merely extraneous additions so the analyzer highlights only actionable errors.",
    "**Sequencing / spec construction**\n- Build the final task spec only from user messages, in order, and treat each later turn as a possible constraint update. Resolve the conversation into one merged, latest-intent spec before judging the assistant, rather than evaluating any intermediate request in isolation.\n- Distinguish a true task-shape change from a simple refinement. If the user changes the deliverable type, granularity, ordering, or level of aggregation, re-derive the spec from that point forward; if they only add a constraint, merge it cumulatively.\n- When the user narrows the request incrementally, preserve earlier constraints unless the user clearly resets or overwrites them. Later instructions often refine presentation or scope without revoking the original content requirements.\n- For iterative or edit-style conversations, compact away exploratory branches and assistant-added assumptions once the user has clarified the target. Keep only the stable user constraints and the currently relevant aligned work.\n- Prefer the narrowest faithful spec supported by the latest user wording. The analyzer should optimize for the final request shape, not the broad theme of the conversation.\n\n**Constraint decomposition**\n- Separate content requirements from output shape. Track target entities, required fields, filters, aggregation level, ordering, and formatting as distinct dimensions so the analyzer can catch cases where the right items are returned in the wrong form.\n- Distinguish row selection from projection. A response can satisfy the filter or join logic and still fail because it returns the wrong columns, granularity, or answer type.\n- Treat the user\u2019s exact scope as authoritative. If they ask for a specific subset, field set, or entity representation, do not weaken it into a semantically related broader answer.\n- When the user asks about presence, membership, or whether something exists, do not silently reinterpret the task as counting, summarizing, or enumerating unless the user explicitly changes the output type.\n\n**Diagnostic patterns**\n- Check both missing and surplus elements against the merged spec. Extra fields, extra alternatives, extra summaries, stale sorting, or unnecessary verbosity can all be divergence, not just omitted requirements.\n- Treat ordering requirements as first-class constraints. A correct content set in the wrong order is still a mismatch.\n- Watch for aggregation-level drift: \u201cshow\u201d versus \u201chow many,\u201d list versus count, detailed rows versus summary, per-item versus overall aggregate. A correct relation can still be the wrong deliverable type.\n- Flag over-specification when the assistant adds context, variants, counts, placeholders, or broader output structure that the user did not request. Extra detail is not automatically harmless if it obscures the required answer shape.\n- Recognize neighboring-problem answers. An output may be logically related and even internally correct, but still fail if it answers a broader or different question than the one the user actually asked.\n- If the user asks for a minimum/maximum or similar extremum, keep explicit whether the target is per-row, per-group, or global. Ambiguity here is a common source of false alignment.\n- Treat semantically equivalent rewrites as insufficient when they change the requested shape. Intent equivalence does not erase mismatches in format, aggregation, or projection.\n\n**Common pitfalls to avoid**\n- Do not let earlier partially correct reasoning mask a later mismatch. A response can satisfy an intermediate step while still failing the final merged user objective.\n- Do not preserve alternative interpretations after the user has clarified. Once ambiguity is resolved, continue with one spec, not a menu of possibilities.\n- Do not reward \u201cdirectionally correct\u201d responses if they omit a required field or return the wrong output form. Partial correctness is not enough when the final deliverable is structurally wrong.\n- Do not overgeneralize from a broader acceptable condition when the user asked for an exact subset, exact enumeration, or exact ordering.\n- Do not treat assistant questions, exploratory branching, or multiple query variants as progress when the user already supplied enough detail. That usually signals drift, not helpfulness.\n- Do not confuse robust internal reasoning with acceptable extra answer content. Extra internal checks may be fine; extra delivered content must still match the user\u2019s requested shape.\n- Do not anchor on the conversation theme. The analyzer must compare the final answer to the user\u2019s latest cumulative intent, not to the broad topic or an earlier broad framing.\n\n**Successful analysis strategies**\n- Use the latest user message as the authoritative update while preserving earlier user constraints that remain active.\n- Compact the conversation into one normalized, executable task spec before evaluating the assistant. This prevents stale broad interpretations from biasing the diagnosis.\n- When the user\u2019s wording shifts from exploration to a concrete target, compare the assistant\u2019s output shape explicitly against the requested one: count vs. list, records vs. summary, minimal output vs. expanded report.\n- Separate reusable correctness from non-reusable output shape. A response may reuse the right substantive logic but still need correction if the final projection, granularity, or ordering changed.\n- In the final assessment, separate true contradictions from merely extraneous additions so the analyzer highlights actionable errors rather than stylistic preferences.\n- Use the derived user intent as the consistency check, not the assistant\u2019s framing. The assistant\u2019s paraphrase should never redefine what the task was."
